<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Final exam problems on the cross product
-->
<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-exercises-cross-product" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Exercises for <xref ref="section-cross-product" text="title"/></title>

<introduction>
<p>
These problems use the cross product and the triple product. If you would like to review this topic before
attempting them, see <xref ref="section-cross-product" text="title"/>.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise xml:id="rw23-9-a">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement correct="no">
<p>
If <m>\vec u</m> and <m>\vec v</m> are two non-zero vectors in
<m>\R^3</m>, then
<m>\vec u \cdot (\vec u \times \vec v)
= \|\vec u\|^2 + \vec u \cdot \vec v</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
Counterexample: take
<m>\vec u = \mathbf i</m> and <m>\vec v = \mathbf i</m>. Then
<md>
\mathbf i \cdot
\underbrace{(\mathbf i \times \mathbf i)}_{\vec 0} = 0,
\quad\text{whereas}\quad
\|\mathbf i\|^2 + \mathbf i \cdot \mathbf i = 1 + 1 = 2,
</md>
and <m>0 \ne 2</m>.
</p>
</feedback>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-8">
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>\mathbf v</m> be the vector
<m>3\,\mathbf i - 4\,\mathbf j + 5\,\mathbf k</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find a non-zero vector <m>\mathbf v'</m> orthogonal to
<m>\mathbf v</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find a vector orthogonal to both <m>\mathbf v</m> and
<m>\mathbf v'</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
We need <m>\langle a,b,c\rangle</m> with
<m>3a - 4b + 5c = 0</m>. Any solution will do; taking <m>c = 0</m>
leaves <m>3a = 4b</m>, so <m>a = 4</m>, <m>b = 3</m> works:
<md>
\mathbf v' = 4\,\mathbf i + 3\,\mathbf j,
\qquad
\mathbf v \cdot \mathbf v' = 12 - 12 + 0 = 0.
</md>
(There are infinitely many correct answers.)
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The cross product is orthogonal to both:
<md>
<mrow>\mathbf v \times \mathbf v' \amp =
\begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf i \amp \mathbf j \amp \mathbf k \\
3 \amp -4 \amp 5 \\
4 \amp 3 \amp 0
\end{vmatrix}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = (0 - 15)\mathbf i - (0 - 20)\mathbf j + (9 + 16)\mathbf k</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -15\,\mathbf i + 20\,\mathbf j + 25\,\mathbf k.</mrow>
</md>
Dividing by <m>5</m> gives the simpler answer
<m>-3\,\mathbf i + 4\,\mathbf j + 5\,\mathbf k</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-13">
<statement>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find the area of the triangle with vertices <m>(0,1,1)</m>,
<m>(-1,2,4)</m>, <m>(3,0,1)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the equation of the plane containing the triangle.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Take <m>A(0,1,1)</m> as the base vertex and form two edge vectors:
<md>
\vec a = \vec{AB} = \langle -1, 1, 3\rangle,
\qquad
\vec b = \vec{AC} = \langle 3, -1, 0\rangle.
</md>
Their cross product is
<md>
\vec a \times \vec b =
\begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf i \amp \mathbf j \amp \mathbf k \\
-1 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\
3 \amp -1 \amp 0
\end{vmatrix}
= (0+3)\mathbf i - (0-9)\mathbf j + (1-3)\mathbf k
= \langle 3, 9, -2\rangle.
</md>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
The magnitude of the cross product is the area of the parallelogram
spanned by <m>\vec a</m> and <m>\vec b</m>, so the triangle has half
that area:
<md>
\text{Area} = \frac12\left|\vec a \times \vec b\right|
= \frac12\sqrt{9 + 81 + 4}
= \frac{\sqrt{94}}{2}.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The cross product is normal to the plane, so the plane has the form
<m>3x + 9y - 2z = D</m>. Substituting the point <m>(0,1,1)</m> gives
<m>D = 0 + 9 - 2 = 7</m>, hence
<md>
3x + 9y - 2z = 7.
</md>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="m2b-4">
<statement>
<p>
Suppose <m>\|\vec A\| = 3</m>, <m>\|\vec B\| = 4</m> and
<m>\|\vec A\times\vec B\| = 6</m>. Find
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
<m>\left|\vec A\cdot\vec B\right|</m>;
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\left\|\operatorname{proj}_{\vec A}\vec B\right\|</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<term>(a)</term> Let <m>\theta</m> be the angle between the two
vectors, <m>0 \le \theta \le \pi</m>. From
<m>\|\vec A\times\vec B\| = \|\vec A\|\,\|\vec B\|\sin\theta</m>,
<md>
6 = (3)(4)\sin\theta \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\sin\theta = \frac{6}{12} = \frac12 .
</md>
Since <m>\sin^2\theta + \cos^2\theta = 1</m>,
<md>
|\cos\theta| = \sqrt{1 - \tfrac14} = \frac{\sqrt3}{2} .
</md>
(We cannot determine the <em>sign</em> of <m>\cos\theta</m>, since
<m>\theta</m> could be <m>30^\circ</m> or <m>150^\circ</m>; this is
exactly why the problem asks for the absolute value.) Therefore
<md>
\left|\vec A\cdot\vec B\right|
 = \|\vec A\|\,\|\vec B\|\,|\cos\theta|
 = (3)(4)\frac{\sqrt3}{2} = 6\sqrt3 .
</md>
</p>

<p>
<term>(b)</term> The projection is
<m>\operatorname{proj}_{\vec A}\vec B
= \left(\dfrac{\vec A\cdot\vec B}{\|\vec A\|^2}\right)\vec A</m>, so
its length is
<md>
\left\|\operatorname{proj}_{\vec A}\vec B\right\|
 = \frac{\left|\vec A\cdot\vec B\right|}{\|\vec A\|^2}\,\|\vec A\|
 = \frac{\left|\vec A\cdot\vec B\right|}{\|\vec A\|}
 = \frac{6\sqrt3}{3} = 2\sqrt3 .
</md>
As a sanity check, <m>2\sqrt3 \approx 3.46</m>, which is less than
<m>\|\vec B\| = 4</m>, as a shadow must be.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="m2b-1c">
<statement>
<p>
Two vectors <m>\vec A</m> and <m>\vec B</m> are drawn with a common
tail; <m>\vec A</m> points up and to the right, <m>\vec B</m> points up
and to the left, and the angle between them is slightly larger than a
right angle. Sketch and clearly label a diagram showing <m>\vec A</m>,
<m>\vec B</m>, and <m>\vec A \times \vec B</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
To make the picture concrete let us take
<m>\vec A = \left\langle 4, 2 \right\rangle</m> and
<m>\vec B = \left\langle -3, 5 \right\rangle</m>, which have roughly
the directions described. Everything below is a statement about
the <em>geometry</em>, so the same picture is correct for any pair of
vectors in that configuration.
</p>

<p>
The cross product <m>\vec A\times\vec B</m> is
perpendicular to both, hence perpendicular to the page. The right-hand
rule (curl the fingers of the right hand from <m>\vec A</m> towards
<m>\vec B</m> through the smaller angle) shows that it points
<em>out of the page</em>, which we draw as a circle with a dot in it.
Indeed, treating the vectors as lying in the <m>xy</m>-plane,
<md>
\vec A\times\vec B
= \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf i \amp \mathbf j \amp \mathbf k \\
4 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
-3 \amp 5 \amp 0
\end{vmatrix}
= \left\langle 0,\ 0,\ (4)(5)-(2)(-3) \right\rangle
= 26\,\mathbf k ,
</md>
and the <m>\mathbf k</m>-component is positive. Its length,
<m>26</m>, is the area of the parallelogram spanned by <m>\vec A</m>
and <m>\vec B</m>. See <xref ref="fig-m2b-1c"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-m2b-1c">
<caption>
<m>\vec A\times\vec B</m> points out of the page, drawn as a circle
with a dot at its centre.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<description>
<p>
A blue arrow A points up and to the right and a green arrow B points
up and to the left from a common tail. A circle with a dot at its
centre represents A cross B, a vector pointing out of the page.
</p>
</description>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-m2b-1c">
<diagram dimensions="(300,290)" margins="16">
<coordinates bbox="(-4.0,-0.8,5.0,8.0)">
<vector at="pc-A" v="(4,2)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="pc-B" v="(-3,5)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#009E73" thickness="2"/>
<circle at="pc-out" center="(-1.4,7.0)" radius="0.62"
        stroke="#8B1A4A" thickness="2.5" fill="none"/>
<point at="pc-dot" p="(-1.4,7.0)" fill="#8B1A4A"/>
<label at="pc-Al" anchor="(4.1,1.9)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>\vec A</m>
</label>
<label at="pc-Bl" anchor="(-3.1,5.1)" alignment="nw" color="#009E73">
<m>\vec B</m>
</label>
<label at="pc-Xl" anchor="(-0.6,7.0)" alignment="e" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>\vec A\times\vec B</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Vectors A and B drawn from a common tail. A circle with a dot at its centre, above and to the left, represents A cross B pointing out of the page.">
  <annotation ref="pc-A" text="The vector A."/>
  <annotation ref="pc-B" text="The vector B."/>
  <annotation ref="pc-out" text="A circle with a dot at its centre, the standard symbol for a vector pointing out of the page, representing A cross B."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

</worksheet>
